WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  president putin has put his nuclear  deterrence forces on high alert i  ordered the minister of defense  and the chief of general staff  to put  determined forces  of the russian army  on special  combat standby duty  that means the kremlin's nuclear weapons  operators will be primed and ready to  attack at short notice if president  putin decides it's necessary it's  basically down to putin whether or not  to launch the nuclear weapons and he has  constant access to a briefcase called  the cheget from which he can launch the  weapons in response to a crisis  do you think he's crazy enough to  instigate a nuclear conflict i'm not  going to speculate on what he would or  wouldn't do but that's why we all keep  our deterrence at a state of readiness  in the west to make sure that we can  deter the world's use of nuclear weapons  is kept in check by a concept called  mutually assured destruction  the idea is fairly simple and works on  the assumption that the risk of  retaliation from one nuclear power  against another would be so devastating  it ensures two nuclear powers would  never in reality attack one another  however the theory doesn't hold for a  nuclear state attacking a non-nuclear  power  globally there are five states  officially recognised as possessing  nuclear weapons by the non-proliferation  treaty that's an international treaty  designed to prevent the spread of  nuclear weapons the five countries are  also the permanent five members of the  un security council china france russia  the us and the united kingdom in january  2022 they all pledged to prevent atomic  weapons from spreading and to ensure a  nuclear war is never fought  there's also a handful of countries that  have nuclear weapons that aren't signed  up to the treaty these are believed to  include india israel pakistan and north  korea  but more than 90 percent of the world's  nuclear warheads belong to russia and  the united states  each country has their own types of  nuclear weapons and different ways to  deliver them the uk's nuclear deterrent  called trident uses four vanguard-class  nuclear-powered submarines this means  that every hour of every day at least  one is at sea ready to launch an attack  if ordered the us uses submarines bomber  planes and intercontinental missiles  which briefly leave the earth's  atmosphere when fired they have more  than five and a half thousand weapons  russia has even more than the us with  more than six 000 nuclear weapons they  can be launched from submarines bomber  planes and intercontinental missiles but  the country also distinguishes between  different types of nuclear warfare in a  way that others don't  vladimir putin's latest nuclear threat  is a departure from established russian  nuclear deterrence doctrine in 2020  putin approved basic principles with  four cases where moscow could use  nuclear weapons  they were when ballistic missiles were  fired at russia or allies territory  when an enemy used nuclear weapons an  attack on a russian nuclear weapons site  or an attack threatening the existence  of the russian state none of those  criteria has been met so far in the  current conflict  but the doctrine also allows for smaller  tactical nuclear warheads to be  threatened in a conventional conflict  including against a non-nuclear armed  country such as ukraine in the hope that  the other side capitulates in fear it's  referred to as escalate to de-escalate  in this context they might use a small  nuclear weapon in ukraine but given nato  has not intervened so far with the  military response this seems very  unlikely ukraine has no nuclear weapons  after decommissioning them in 1994 in  return for security guarantees now being  ripped up by russia in return for  decommissioning their nuclear weapons  which they inherited when the ussr  disintegrated they were told russia and  the west would guarantee their security  this was called the budapest memorandum  nuclear weapons have only been used  twice over hiroshima and nagasaki during  the second world war  the devastation and suffering they  caused helped bring the second world war  to an end but have spurred calls for  disarmament in the years ever since  putin's warned interference in his  military campaign in ukraine could lead  to consequences that you have never  encountered in your history as he put it  widely regarded as a thinly veiled  threat to use nuclear weapons  that in his willingness to put his  nuclear weapons on increased readiness  so early in the conflict is causing  considerable concern  in the west as with so much of this  conflict it lies in the hands of  vladimir putin

